  "assessment_summary": "This paper investigates the intergenerational impact of a school-based deworming program (PSDP) in Kenya on child mortality, using a 23-year longitudinal dataset from the Kenya Life Panel Survey (KLPS). The central claim is that the deworming intervention led to a 22% reduction in under-5 mortality among children of those who received the treatment. The study explores potential mechanisms through which deworming may have contributed to this reduction, including improvements in parent health, education, living standards, fertility patterns, and healthcare access. A cost-benefit analysis is also conducted to assess the economic value of increased child survival due to deworming. The research leverages the experimental design of the PSDP and the long-term panel data of the KLPS, offering a unique opportunity to study the intergenerational transmission of health outcomes. However, several issues must be considered when evaluating the study's findings. These include potential selection bias due to sample attrition, the challenges of disentangling the causal effects of multiple mechanisms, and the limitations of relying on self-reported data. While the authors attempt to address these concerns through robustness checks and sensitivity analyses, some uncertainty remains regarding the magnitude and interpretation of the results. A key concern is the external validity of the findings, as the KLPS sample is not nationally representative and is drawn from a specific region in Kenya. The long follow up period however allows a better understanding of longer term impacts, the cost-benefit analysis does rely on willingness to pay estimates which might be context specific. The dose response analysis provides further evidence of the interventions effects.",
